rootCause:
Not one bug: the delay is built into the design. Ranked by likely size (none of it measured yet):

1. The proxy body is never flushed (Ktor 3 non-autoFlush channel, CloudStreamProxy.kt:365). Every uncached start and every seek waits for about 1 MiB plus two extra googlevideo round trips before ExoPlayer gets a byte. Estimated cost:
   - about 0.2-0.5 s on fast Wi-Fi;
   - about 1 s at 10 Mbps;
   - about 3 s at 3 Mbps.
2. Upcoming songs get no audio bytes ahead of time:
   - for Spotify, only the next song's URL is warmed, depth 1;
   - ExoPlayer's own next-item loading covers only about the last 60 s;
   - a skip throws away crossfade's prepared playerB.
3. The full-song auto-cache download of the same song (and of both neighbours) starts at the tap and shares bandwidth with the first bytes (DualPlayerEngine.kt:1304).
4. ExoPlayer waits for 2.5 s of buffered audio instead of Media3's 1 s.
5. The first resolve of each process pays several one-off costs:
   - the blocking visitorData NewPipe call;
   - the lazy proxy start;
   - if the URL carries `n`: base.js downloaded per process, plus a WebView that loads youtube.com.
6. YouTube clients and Spotify on-demand matching both run strictly one after another (YouTubeStreamResolver.kt:262-264; TrackMatcher.kt:56-64).

recommendedPlan.change:
Prerequisite for the next two steps: the auto-cache must never compete with the first bytes and must never fire on speculative opens.
- Remove `audioCacheManager.maybeAutoCache(spotifyId)` from resolveCloudUri. Today it fires on:
  - the UI's pre-play call;
  - every ExoPlayer open and re-open;
  - the 600 ms neighbour pre-resolution;
  - and, after step 6, it would fire on every ExoPlayer preload.
- New trigger in MusicService: a job started in onMediaItemTransition / onIsPlayingChanged(true) and cancelled on change. After the LOCAL engine.masterPlayer has played the current spotify:// item for 5 s, it calls maybeAutoCache(id), except under Data Saver (owner decision).
- Neighbours:
  - drop the previous-item leg;
  - give the next item (shuffle order) its full-song cache only on unmetered networks with Data Saver off, after the current song's job (owner decision);
  - the 600 ms pre-resolution then does only cheap work (proxy start plus a DB read).
- Fix buildResolvedPlaybackMediaItem's comment: it does not resolve YouTube.

R3, prepare the upcoming songs following the owner's rules: next 1 on cellular, next 2 on Wi-Fi, nothing under Data Saver, only while playing.
- Add a pure `object StreamPrefetchPolicy` that mirrors iOS PixlNet StreamPrefetchPolicy:
  - `enum class Network { WIFI, ETHERNET, CELLULAR, OTHER, OFFLINE }`
  - `data class Conditions(network, metered: Boolean, dataSaver: Boolean)`
  - `depth(c, isPlaying, isConnectSession)`: 0 when not playing, in a Connect session, under Data Saver or offline; 2 on WIFI/ETHERNET when not metered; otherwise 1.
  - `upcomingIndices(current, depth, next: (Int) -> Int)`: steps with `timeline.getNextWindowIndex(i, repeatMode, shuffleModeEnabled)`, stops at C.INDEX_UNSET, never repeats an index or returns the current one.
- Read the conditions at decision time:
  - `ConnectivityManager.getNetworkCapabilities(activeNetwork)`: TRANSPORT_WIFI / ETHERNET / CELLULAR; metered = !(NET_CAPABILITY_NOT_METERED || NET_CAPABILITY_TEMPORARILY_NOT_METERED), the latter API 30, which is minSdk;
  - dataSaver = `getRestrictBackgroundStatus() == ConnectivityManager.RESTRICT_BACKGROUND_STATUS_ENABLED`;
  - ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE is already declared (AndroidManifest.xml:6).
- Changes to updateNextStreamPrefetch:
  - read isPlaying and the timeline from engine.masterPlayer (local), not mediaSession.player;
  - isConnectSession = connectSessionPlayer != null;
  - candidates are the spotify ids from upcomingIndices that have no playable file;
  - keep the 1.5 s settle and the existing call sites.
- StreamUrlPrefetcher gains `prefetch(ids: List<K>)`:
  - resolves the ids sequentially, in order;
  - the same list again is a no-op; a different list cancels the running job;
  - CloudStreamProxy exposes it as `prefetchStreamUrls(ids)`.
  The +2 song is warmed once (match + URL). The 60 s loop re-checks only the +1.
- Raise the speculative TTL at :85 from 120 s to 10 min. It is still capped by the signed expire − 60 s (:80-82), and a 403 refreshes through :260-279.

R8 behind a remote flag that defaults OFF.
- StreamRemoteFlags (@Singleton) reads only the `innertube.hedge` object. iOS just added the same object and clamps (ios-wt/stream RemoteClientConfig.swift `hedging`):
  - `enabled`;
  - `afterSeconds`, clamped 0.5-10, default 1.5;
  - `strategyTimeoutSeconds`, clamped 3-15, default 8.
- Parse with kotlinx.serialization.json (`Json.parseToJsonElement`); org.json is stubbed in JVM tests.
- Fetch:
  - in the background at most every 6 h, cached in filesDir/stream-remote-flags.json;
  - `current()` never waits;
  - URL per owner decision; suggested: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/RedSn0w1877/PixlAudio-iOS/main/remote/config.json. Android ignores that file's iOS profiles.
- resolveStream:
  - hedging == null keeps today's loop unchanged;
  - otherwise a pure `HedgedRace` runs `coroutineScope` + `Channel`: start strategy 0; start i+1 when the newest one fails or after afterMs; each strategy under `withTimeoutOrNull(strategyTimeoutMs)`; the first non-blank URL wins and `coroutineContext.cancelChildren()` stops the rest. awaitYouTubeResponse cancels the OkHttp calls (CancellableYouTubeCall.kt:11-21).
  - Attempts are recorded in completion order.
- Note: InnerTubeClient.lastFailureReason (:81-83) is shared, so a losing client's detail can be wrong (diagnostics only).

Plan the port with measurements.
- Builds:
  - A = step 1 only (baseline);
  - B = + steps 2-3;
  - C = + steps 4-6;
  - D = + steps 7 and 9, with the flag off, then on.
- Run each on Wi-Fi and on 5G, Data Saver off and on, 10 runs per scenario:
  - S1: cold-process first Spotify tap;
  - S2: warm tap on an uncached song;
  - S3: manual skip about 20 s into a song;
  - S4: auto-advance;
  - S5: seek to 70% of an uncached song;
  - S6: replay of an auto-cached song (control).
- Read the 'Last stream start' line in the Playback test card, or `adb logcat -s StreamStart`. Ask the owner before installing on or driving the phone (AGENTS.md).
- Record p50/p90 of:
  - tap→playing;
  - URL time (match / resolve / client / n);
  - upstream TTFB;
  - player first byte − upstream first byte (the flush gap);
  - ready time;
  - bytes per prepared-but-unplayed song;
  - InnerTube player calls per played song;
  - early rebuffers.
- Expected:
  - B: the flush gap drops to under 50 ms. Tap→playing falls by about 1 MiB/bandwidth + 2 round trips, plus up to 1.5 s of audio no longer needed before start.
  - C: S3/S4 p50 under 300 ms, with a 'preload hit' and no player proxy request after the transition; at most about 1 MiB per prepared song on cellular.
  - S1 shows the cold costs (visitorData, proxy start, base.js/WebView if `n`), which decide step 8 and whether to turn R8 on.
